/-!
# The dual of an invertible sheaf is the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme of a regular section

Let `X` be a scheme, `L` an invertible `𝒪_X`-module and `s ∈ Γ(X, L)` a global section whose
zero scheme ([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]) is `E = Z(s)`. Evaluation at `s` is a morphism

`σ : L^∨ ⟶ 𝒪_X`,

and the defining ideal of `E` on an affine open is by construction the ideal generated by the
values of `σ` there. So `σ` factors through the ideal sheaf `ℐ_E ⊆ 𝒪_X` and is surjective onto
it; when `s` is a nonzerodivisor ([Stacks, Tag 01WY]) the factorization is injective as well,
and `L^∨ ≅ ℐ_E`.

Dualizing that identification and composing with the canonical map to the double dual — which
is an isomorphism because `L` is invertible, hence locally free of rank one — gives an
isomorphism `L ≅ 𝒪_X(E)` under which `s` becomes the canonical section `1_E`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.dualEvalLinearMap`: a section of `L^∨` over an open, read as a linear
  functional on the sections of `L` there.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.dualEvalHom`: the morphism `σ : L^∨ ⟶ 𝒪_X` given by evaluation at a
  global section `s` of `L`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroSchemeIdealHom`: the factorization `L^∨ ⟶ ℐ_{Z(s)}` of `σ` through
  the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_zeroSchemeIdealHom` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom`: for a nonzerodivisor section the
  factorization is an isomorphism, `L^∨ ≅ ℐ_{Z(s)}`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_toDoubleDual_of_isInvertible`: an invertible module is reflexive.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme_section`: the isomorphism
  `L ≅ 𝒪_X(Z(s))` and the fact that it carries `s` to the canonical section.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection_section`: the same two
  statements with the hypothesis written as `Stacks.Divisors.IsRegularSection`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_app_eq_of_locally`: a morphism of sheaves of modules which is
  injective on sections and hits a section locally hits it. This is the only use of the gluing
  half of the sheaf axiom in this file.

## Implementation notes

**Which dual.** The library carries two spellings of the dual of a sheaf of modules:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.dualSheaf`, indexed by a presheaf of commutative rings together with a
sheaf condition, and `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.dualSheaf`, indexed by a
scheme. The second is used here, because the sections of its argument and of its value are
both written in the `Γ(-, -)` notation of `Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/Modules/Sheaf.lean` and
the evaluation pairing `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.evalAt` and the canonical map
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.toDoubleDual` are stated there. The two agree:
`Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' D` is the first dual of `Stacks.Divisors.negativeTwist' D` and
the second dual of the same sheaf, and the identification is definitional, which is what
`AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme` relies on to land in the shape
[Stacks, Tag 01WX] states.

**Two categories of sheaves of modules.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` carries its own
`Category` instance, whose `Hom` field is a definitional copy of `SheafOfModules.Hom`, so a
morphism of `X.Modules` is a morphism of `SheafOfModules X.ringCatSheaf` and conversely; but
instance search does not cross between the two spellings, and a morphism whose source is
written at the first index does not get the zero morphisms and the kernels of the second.
`AlgebraicGeometry.dualModule` and `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafModule` fix the two spellings
once each, and everything that has to be an isomorphism is shown to be one through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.Hom.isIso_iff_isIso_app`, which asks only for bijectivity on
sections and is stated for `X.Modules`.

Bijectivity on the sections over *every* open — rather than local bijectivity plus a balanced
category — is what `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_app_eq_of_locally` is for: the local preimages
under an injective morphism of sheaves are unique, hence compatible, hence glue.

**Why a rank-one dual basis and not a linear equivalence on an affine open.** A trivialization
of `L` over an open in the form of a linear equivalence `Γ(L, U) ≃ₗ Γ(X, U)` says nothing about
the sections of `L` over the open subsets of `U`, which is what a section of `L^∨` over `U`
sees. `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne` is the trivialization over the whole slice site, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_bijective_smul_of_isInvertible` with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.ofIsoUnit` produces one around every point of an invertible
module. The local computations below all take place over such an open.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WX)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OQ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OR)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace Opposite
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-- **The dual `L^∨` of an `𝒪_X`-module, read in the category of sheaves of modules over the
structure sheaf of the underlying ringed space.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` has its own
`Category` instance, and the ideal sheaf of a closed subscheme is a kernel taken in the other
one; the two categories have the same objects and the same morphisms, but a morphism whose
source is spelled at the first index does not get the zero morphisms and the kernels of the
second by instance search. Naming the dual at the second index fixes that spelling once. -/
abbrev dualModule (L : X.Modules) :
    SheafOfModules.{u} (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace) :=
  dualSheaf L

/-- **The ideal sheaf `ℐ_D` of a closed subscheme, read as an object of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules`.** This is `Stacks.Divisors.negativeTwist'`; only the spelling
of its type differs, and that is what makes a morphism with it as source or target live in the
category `X.Modules`, where the dual functor
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.dualMap` is defined. -/
abbrev idealSheafModule (I : X.IdealSheafData) : X.Modules := Stacks.Divisors.negativeTwist' I

/-! ### Evaluation at a global section -/

/-- **A section of the dual `L^∨` over an open, read as a linear functional on the sections of
`L` there.** Its value at a section of `L` is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.evalAt`. -/
def dualEvalLinearMap (L : X.Modules) {U : X.Opens} (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    Γ(L, U) →ₗ[Γ(X, U)] Γ(X, U) where
  toFun x := evalAt L t x
  map_add' x y := evalAt_add_right L t x y
  map_smul' a x := evalAt_smul_right L a t x

/-- **Evaluation at a global section commutes with restriction.** Restricting a functional and
evaluating it at the restricted section gives the restriction of the value. -/
theorem evalAt_res_top (L : X.Modules) (s : Γ(L, (⊤ : X.Opens))) {U W : X.Opens} (h : W ≤ U)
    (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    evalAt L ((dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE h).op t)
        (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) =
      X.presheaf.map (homOfLE h).op (evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)) := by
  have h3 : L.presheaf.map (homOfLE h).op (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : U ≤ ⊤)).op s) =
      L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W ≤ ⊤)).op s := by
    rw [← L.presheaf.map_comp_apply]
    congr 1
  rw [← h3]
  exact evalAt_map L h t _

/-- **Evaluation at a global section `s` of `L`**, as a morphism `σ : L^∨ ⟶ 𝒪_X` of
`𝒪_X`-modules: over an open `U` it sends a functional `t` on `L|_U` to its value at `s|_U`. -/
def dualEvalHom (L : X.Modules) (s : Γ(L, (⊤ : X.Opens))) :
    dualSheaf L ⟶ (SheafOfModules.unit (structureSheaf X.sheaf) : X.Modules) where
  val :=
    { app := fun U => ModuleCat.homAddEquiv.symm
        { toFun := fun t => evalAt L (V := U.unop) t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)
          map_add' := fun t t' => evalAt_add L t t' _
          map_smul' := fun a t => evalAt_smul L a t _ }
      naturality := fun {U V} i => by
        apply ModuleCat.hom_ext
        apply LinearMap.ext
        intro t
        have hle : V.unop ≤ U.unop := leOfHom i.unop
        have hi : i = (homOfLE hle).op := Subsingleton.elim _ _
        subst hi
        have h3 : L.presheaf.map (homOfLE hle).op (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) =
            L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : V.unop ≤ ⊤)).op s := by
          rw [← L.presheaf.map_comp_apply]
          congr 1
        change evalAt L (V := V.unop) ((dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE hle).op t)
            (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) = _
        rw [← h3]
        exact evalAt_map L hle t _ }

/-- Evaluation at a global section, read off on sections over an open. -/
theorem dualEvalHom_app (L : X.Modules) (s : Γ(L, (⊤ : X.Opens))) (U : X.Opens)
    (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (dualEvalHom L s) U t =
      evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) := rfl

/-! ### Gluing preimages under an injective morphism -/

/-- Restricting a local preimage: if `m` over `W` has image the restriction of `n`, then the
restriction of `m` to a smaller open has image the restriction of `n` there. -/
private theorem app_resLE_of_app_eq {T : TopCat.{u}} {O : (Opens T)ᵒᵖ ⥤ RingCat.{u}}
    {M N : PresheafOfModules.{u} O} (φ : M ⟶ N) {V W : Opens T} (hWV : W ≤ V)
    {n : N.obj (op V)} {m : M.obj (op W)} (hm : φ.app (op W) m = N.resLE hWV n)
    {Z : Opens T} (hZ : Z ≤ W) :
    φ.app (op Z) (M.resLE hZ m) = N.resLE (hZ.trans hWV) n := by
  rw [PresheafOfModules.app_resLE φ hZ m, hm, PresheafOfModules.resLE_resLE]

/-- **A morphism of sheaves of modules which is injective on sections, and which hits a section
locally, hits it.** The local preimages are unique by injectivity, hence agree on overlaps,
hence glue; and the glued preimage has the right image because the target is separated.

This is the only place in this file where the sheaf axiom is used in its gluing form, and it is
what turns the affine-local computation of the ideal of a zero scheme into a statement about
sections over an arbitrary open. -/
theorem exists_app_eq_of_locally {T : TopCat.{u}} {O : (Opens T)ᵒᵖ ⥤ RingCat.{u}}
    {M N : PresheafOfModules.{u} O}
    (hM : CategoryTheory.Presheaf.IsSheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology (T : Type u)) M.presheaf)
    (hN : CategoryTheory.Presheaf.IsSheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology (T : Type u)) N.presheaf)
    (φ : M ⟶ N) (hinj : ∀ V : Opens T, Function.Injective fun m => φ.app (op V) m)
    {V : Opens T} (n : N.obj (op V))
    (hloc : ∀ y ∈ V, ∃ (W : Opens T) (hWV : W ≤ V), y ∈ W ∧
      ∃ m : M.obj (op W), φ.app (op W) m = N.resLE hWV n) :
    ∃ m : M.obj (op V), φ.app (op V) m = n := by
  classical
  choose W hWV hyW m hm using hloc
  set K : Type u := {y : T // y ∈ V} with hK
  set W' : K → Opens T := fun k => W k.1 k.2 with hW'
  have hW'V : ∀ k, W' k ≤ V := fun k => hWV k.1 k.2
  have hcov : V ≤ ⨆ k, W' k := fun y hy =>
    TopologicalSpace.Opens.mem_iSup.2 ⟨⟨y, hy⟩, hyW y hy⟩
  have hcompat : ∀ k l : K, M.resLE (inf_le_left : W' k ⊓ W' l ≤ W' k) (m k.1 k.2) =
      M.resLE (inf_le_right : W' k ⊓ W' l ≤ W' l) (m l.1 l.2) :=
    fun k l => hinj (W' k ⊓ W' l)
      ((app_resLE_of_app_eq φ (hW'V k) (hm k.1 k.2) inf_le_left).trans
        (app_resLE_of_app_eq φ (hW'V l) (hm l.1 l.2) inf_le_right).symm)
  obtain ⟨t, ht, -⟩ := TopCat.Sheaf.existsUnique_gluing_of_isSheafPresheaf hM
    W' hW'V hcov (fun k => m k.1 k.2) hcompat
  refine ⟨t, TopCat.Sheaf.eq_of_locally_eq_of_isSheafPresheaf hN W' hW'V hcov fun k => ?_⟩
  rw [← PresheafOfModules.app_resLE φ (hW'V k) t, ht k, hm k.1 k.2]

/-! ### Rank-one dual bases -/

/-- **The basis section of a rank-one dual basis of `L` over `W`, in the `Γ(-, -)` spelling.**
This is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.e`; only the spelling of its type differs, and that
is what makes the action of `Γ(X, W)` on it resolve by instance search. -/
def dualBasisSection (L : X.Modules) {W : X.Opens}
    (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L W) : Γ(L, W) := b.e

/-- **The dual coordinate of a rank-one dual basis of `L` over `W`, in the `Γ(-, -)`
spelling.** This is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.lam`; only the spelling of its type
differs, and that is what makes the action of `Γ(X, W)` on it resolve by instance search. -/
def dualBasisCoord (L : X.Modules) {W : X.Opens}
    (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L W) : Γ(dualSheaf L, W) := b.lam

/-- Evaluation at a fixed section is injective on functionals once it has trivial kernel. -/
private theorem injective_evalAt_of_eq_zero (L : X.Modules) {U : X.Opens} (g : Γ(L, U))
    (h : ∀ t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U), evalAt L t g = 0 → t = 0) :
    Function.Injective fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U) => evalAt L t g :=
  (injective_iff_map_eq_zero
    (AddMonoidHom.mk' (fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U) => evalAt L t g)
      fun a b => evalAt_add L a b g)).2 h

section DualBasis

variable {L : X.Modules} {W : X.Opens}
  (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
    (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
    L W)

/-- A section of `L` over `W` is its own coordinate times the basis section. -/
theorem smul_dualBasisSection (g : Γ(L, W)) :
    evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) g • dualBasisSection L b = g := b.coord_smul g

/-- The dual coordinate takes the value `1` on the basis section. -/
theorem evalAt_dualBasisSection :
    evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (dualBasisSection L b) = 1 := b.eval_self

/-- **A functional on `L|_W` is determined by its value on the basis section**: it is the
coordinate times that value. -/
theorem evalAt_eq_mul_evalAt_dualBasisSection (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, W)) (g : Γ(L, W)) :
    evalAt L t g = evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) g * evalAt L t (dualBasisSection L b) :=
  (congrArg (fun y : Γ(L, W) => evalAt L t y) (smul_dualBasisSection b g)).symm.trans
    ((evalAt_smul_right L _ t _).trans (smul_eq_mul _ _))

/-- A section of `L` over `W` whose coordinate vanishes is zero. -/
theorem eq_zero_of_evalAt_dualBasisOne_eq_zero (g : Γ(L, W))
    (h : evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) g = 0) : g = 0 := by
  rw [← smul_dualBasisSection b g, h, zero_smul]

/-- The coordinate map of a rank-one dual basis is injective on sections over `W`. -/
theorem injective_evalAt_dualBasisOne :
    Function.Injective (fun g : Γ(L, W) => evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) g) := by
  exact (injective_iff_map_eq_zero (dualEvalLinearMap L (dualBasisCoord L b))).2
    (eq_zero_of_evalAt_dualBasisOne_eq_zero b)

/-- The coordinate map of a rank-one dual basis hits every section of the structure sheaf. -/
theorem evalAt_smul_dualBasisSection (r : Γ(X, W)) :
    evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (r • dualBasisSection L b) = r :=
  (evalAt_smul_right L r (dualBasisCoord L b) (dualBasisSection L b)).trans
    (by rw [evalAt_dualBasisSection, smul_eq_mul, mul_one])

/-- **A rank-one dual basis trivializes `L` over `W`.** The coordinate map is a linear
equivalence `Γ(L, W) ≃ₗ Γ(X, W)`; this is the form in which
`AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme_ideal_of_trivialization` consumes a trivialization. -/
def dualBasisOneCoordEquiv : Γ(L, W) ≃ₗ[Γ(X, W)] Γ(X, W) :=
  LinearEquiv.ofBijective (dualEvalLinearMap L (dualBasisCoord L b))
    ⟨injective_evalAt_dualBasisOne b,
      fun r => ⟨r • dualBasisSection L b, evalAt_smul_dualBasisSection b r⟩⟩

/-- The trivialization attached to a rank-one dual basis is the coordinate map. -/
theorem dualBasisOneCoordEquiv_apply (g : Γ(L, W)) :
    dualBasisOneCoordEquiv b g = evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) g := rfl

/-- The coordinate of a rank-one dual basis is injective as a functional on the dual: a
section of `L^∨` over `W` killing the basis section is zero. -/
theorem injective_evalAt_dualBasisSection :
    Function.Injective fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, W) => evalAt L t (dualBasisSection L b) := by
  exact injective_evalAt_of_eq_zero L _ fun t => b.eq_zero_of_eval_e t

/-- **A rank-one dual basis of `L` over `W` makes `L^∨|_W` free of rank one on the dual
coordinate.** A section of the dual is its value on the basis section times the coordinate. -/
theorem smul_dualBasisCoord (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, W)) :
    evalAt L t (dualBasisSection L b) • dualBasisCoord L b = t := by
  refine injective_evalAt_dualBasisSection b ?_
  change evalAt L (evalAt L t (dualBasisSection L b) • dualBasisCoord L b)
      (dualBasisSection L b) = evalAt L t (dualBasisSection L b)
  rw [evalAt_smul, evalAt_dualBasisSection, smul_eq_mul, mul_one]

/-- The basis section of a restricted rank-one dual basis is the restricted basis section. -/
theorem dualBasisSection_restrict {V : X.Opens} (h : V ≤ W) :
    dualBasisSection L (b.restrict (homOfLE h)) =
      L.presheaf.map (homOfLE h).op (dualBasisSection L b) := rfl

/-- The dual coordinate of a restricted rank-one dual basis is the restricted coordinate. -/
theorem dualBasisCoord_restrict {V : X.Opens} (h : V ≤ W) :
    dualBasisCoord L (b.restrict (homOfLE h)) =
      (dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE h).op (dualBasisCoord L b) := rfl

end DualBasis

/-- **An invertible module has a rank-one dual basis on a neighbourhood of every point.** This
is the converse half of [Stacks, Tag 0B8M] in the form
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_bijective_smul_of_isInvertible`, turned into a dual basis by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.ofIsoUnit`. -/
theorem exists_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible (L : X.Modules)
    [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] (x : X) :
    ∃ V : X.Opens, x ∈ V ∧ Nonempty (Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L V) := by
  have hL : Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.presheaf
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L :=
    (Stacks.SheafOfModules.isInvertible_iff_algebraicGeometry _ _ L).2 ‹_›
  obtain ⟨V, hxV, a, ha⟩ :=
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_bijective_smul_of_isInvertible (X := X.toRingedSpace) L x
  exact ⟨V, hxV, ⟨Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.ofIsoUnit
    (Stacks.SheafOfModules.isoUnitOver L V a ha)⟩⟩

/-- **Every point of an open has a neighbourhood inside it which is affine and over which `L`
has a rank-one dual basis.** The dual basis of
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible` is restricted to an affine open of the
basis of the topology (`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne.restrict`). -/
theorem exists_affineOpen_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible (L : X.Modules)
    [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] {U : X.Opens} (x : X)
    (hxU : x ∈ U) :
    ∃ (V : X.affineOpens) (_ : x ∈ (V : X.Opens)) (_ : (V : X.Opens) ≤ U),
      Nonempty (Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
        (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
        L (V : X.Opens)) := by
  obtain ⟨V₀, hxV₀, ⟨b₀⟩⟩ := exists_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible L x
  obtain ⟨-, ⟨V, hV, rfl⟩, hxV, hVle⟩ :=
    X.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open (show x ∈ U ⊓ V₀ from ⟨hxU, hxV₀⟩)
      (U ⊓ V₀).isOpen
  exact ⟨⟨V, hV⟩, hxV, fun y hy => (hVle hy).1,
    ⟨b₀.restrict (homOfLE fun y hy => (hVle hy).2)⟩⟩

/-! ### Reflexivity of an invertible module -/

/-- **The dual of an isomorphism of `𝒪_X`-modules.** The dual
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules.dualMap` is contravariantly functorial, so it carries an
isomorphism to an isomorphism. -/
def dualMapIso {F G : X.Modules} (e : F ≅ G) : dualSheaf G ≅ dualSheaf F where
  hom := dualMap e.hom
  inv := dualMap e.inv
  hom_inv_id := by rw [← dualMap_comp, e.inv_hom_id, dualMap_id]
  inv_hom_id := by rw [← dualMap_comp, e.hom_inv_id, dualMap_id]

section DoubleDual

variable (L : X.Modules)

section Trivialized

variable {V : X.Opens}
  (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
    (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
    L V)

/-- The value of the image under the canonical map to the double dual of a multiple of the basis
section of a rank-one dual basis. -/
theorem toDoubleDual_pairing_smul (c : Γ(X, V)) (W : Over V) (lam : Γ(dualSheaf L, W.left)) :
    dualPairing (dualSheaf L)
        (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) V (c • dualBasisSection L b)) W lam =
      X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom)).op c *
        evalAt L lam (dualBasisSection L (b.restrict (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom)))) := by
  rw [toDoubleDual_dualPairing, presheaf_map_hom_eq L _ W, Scheme.Modules.map_smul]
  exact (evalAt_smul_right L _ lam _).trans (smul_eq_mul _ _)

/-- **A functional on `L^∨|_V` is determined by its value on the dual coordinate** of a rank-one
dual basis: `L^∨|_V` is free of rank one on that coordinate. -/
theorem dualPairing_eq_mul_evalAt (μ : Γ(dualSheaf (dualSheaf L), V)) (W : Over V)
    (lam : Γ(dualSheaf L, W.left)) :
    dualPairing (dualSheaf L) μ W lam =
      evalAt L lam (dualBasisSection L (b.restrict (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom)))) *
        X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom)).op
          (evalAt (dualSheaf L) μ (dualBasisCoord L b)) := by
  refine (dualPairing_terminal (dualSheaf L) μ W lam).symm.trans ?_
  conv_lhs => rw [← smul_dualBasisCoord (b.restrict (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom))) lam]
  refine (evalAt_smul_right (dualSheaf L) _ _ _).trans ?_
  rw [smul_eq_mul]
  exact congrArg
    (fun z => evalAt L lam (dualBasisSection L (b.restrict (homOfLE (leOfHom W.hom)))) * z)
    (evalAt_map (dualSheaf L) (leOfHom W.hom) μ (dualBasisCoord L b))

/-- **Over an open carrying a rank-one dual basis, the canonical map to the double dual is
surjective on sections**: the functional `μ` is the image of its value on the dual coordinate
times the basis section. -/
theorem toDoubleDual_app_smul_dualBasisSection (μ : Γ(dualSheaf (dualSheaf L), V)) :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) V
      (evalAt (dualSheaf L) μ (dualBasisCoord L b) • dualBasisSection L b) = μ := by
  refine dualPairing_ext (F := dualSheaf L) fun W lam => ?_
  rw [toDoubleDual_pairing_smul, dualPairing_eq_mul_evalAt L b μ W lam, mul_comm]

end Trivialized

variable [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]

/-- A section of an invertible module killed by the canonical map to the double dual is zero:
over a trivializing open its coordinate in a rank-one dual basis is one of the values that are
killed. -/
theorem eq_zero_of_toDoubleDual_eq_zero {U : X.Opens} (x : Γ(L, U))
    (h : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) U x = 0) : x = 0 := by
  refine Stacks.SheafOfModules.eq_zero_of_locally_zero' L x fun y hy => ?_
  obtain ⟨V, hyV, hVU, ⟨b⟩⟩ := exists_affineOpen_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible L y hy
  refine ⟨(V : X.Opens), homOfLE hVU, hyV, ?_⟩
  have hval : evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op x) = 0 :=
    congrArg (fun t : Γ(dualSheaf (dualSheaf L), U) =>
      dualPairing (dualSheaf L) t (Over.mk (homOfLE hVU)) (dualBasisCoord L b)) h
  exact eq_zero_of_evalAt_dualBasisOne_eq_zero b _ hval

/-- The canonical map of an invertible module to its double dual is injective on sections over
every open. -/
theorem injective_toDoubleDual_app (U : X.Opens) :
    Function.Injective fun x : Γ(L, U) => Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) U x := by
  exact (injective_iff_map_eq_zero (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) U).hom).2
    (eq_zero_of_toDoubleDual_eq_zero L)

/-- **An invertible module is reflexive**: the canonical map `L ⟶ L^∨∨` is an isomorphism.

It is injective on sections because a rank-one dual basis of `L` over a trivializing open
provides a functional separating the sections there, and surjective because over such an open
both `L` and `L^∨∨` are free of rank one on matching generators; the local preimages are unique,
so they glue. -/
@[ega "IV 18.5.1" "special case"]
theorem isIso_toDoubleDual_of_isInvertible : IsIso (toDoubleDual L) := by
  refine Scheme.Modules.Hom.isIso_iff_isIso_app.mpr fun U => ?_
  refine (ConcreteCategory.isIso_iff_bijective _).mpr
    ⟨injective_toDoubleDual_app L U, fun μ => ?_⟩
  refine exists_app_eq_of_locally L.isSheaf (dualSheaf (dualSheaf L)).isSheaf
    (toDoubleDual L).val (fun V => injective_toDoubleDual_app L V) μ fun y hy => ?_
  obtain ⟨V, hyV, hVU, ⟨b⟩⟩ := exists_affineOpen_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible L y hy
  exact ⟨(V : X.Opens), hVU, hyV, _, toDoubleDual_app_smul_dualBasisSection L b _⟩

end DoubleDual

/-! ### The factorization through the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme -/

section ZeroScheme

variable (L : X.Modules)
  [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]
  (s : Γ(L, (⊤ : X.Opens)))

/-- **The values of `σ` on an affine open lie in the defining ideal of the zero scheme.** That
ideal is by construction generated by the values of the linear functionals on `Γ(L, U)` at
`s|_U` ([Stacks, Tag 02OQ], `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme_ideal`), and a section of `L^∨` over
`U` is one of those functionals. -/
theorem evalAt_mem_zeroScheme_ideal (V : X.affineOpens)
    (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, (V : X.Opens))) :
    evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) ∈ (zeroScheme X L s).ideal V := by
  rw [zeroScheme_ideal X L s V]
  exact Ideal.subset_span ⟨dualEvalLinearMap L t, rfl⟩

/-- Over an affine open, the closed immersion of the zero scheme annihilates the values of
`σ`. -/
theorem subschemeι_app_dualEvalHom_app_eq_zero (V : X.affineOpens)
    (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, (V : X.Opens))) :
    (zeroScheme X L s).subschemeι.app (V : X.Opens)
        (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (dualEvalHom L s) (V : X.Opens) t) = 0 :=
  (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeι_app_eq_zero_iff_exists_idealSheafSection _ _).2
    ((Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection _ V _).1
      (evalAt_mem_zeroScheme_ideal L s V t))

/-- **Evaluation at `s`, read in the category of sheaves of modules over the structure sheaf of
the underlying ringed space.** `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules` carries its own `Category`
instance, and the ideal sheaf of a closed subscheme is a kernel taken in the other one; the two
categories have the same objects and the same morphisms, so this is only a retyping. -/
abbrev dualEvalShHom :
    dualModule L ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace) :=
  dualEvalHom L s

/-- **The values of `σ` are sections of the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme.** Over an affine
open this is the bridge `Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection` applied to
`AlgebraicGeometry.evalAt_mem_zeroScheme_ideal`; the affine opens form a basis of the topology
and the inclusion of the ideal sheaf is injective on sections, so the local preimages glue. -/
theorem exists_idealSheafSection_evalAt (U : X.Opens) (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    ∃ y, Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s) y =
      evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) := by
  refine exists_app_eq_of_locally
    (Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafOfModules (zeroScheme X L s)).isSheaf
    (SheafOfModules.unit (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace)).isSheaf
    (kernel.ι (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s))).val
    (fun V => Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection_injective _) _ fun y hy => ?_
  obtain ⟨-, ⟨W, hW, rfl⟩, hyW, hWU⟩ :=
    X.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open hy U.isOpen
  refine ⟨W, hWU, hyW, ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := (Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection _ ⟨W, hW⟩ _).1
    (evalAt_mem_zeroScheme_ideal L s ⟨W, hW⟩ ((dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE hWU).op t))
  exact ⟨z, hz.trans (evalAt_res_top L s hWU t)⟩

/-- **Evaluation at `s` is killed by the structure-sheaf map of the zero scheme.** Its values
are sections of the ideal sheaf, and the ideal sheaf is the kernel of that map. -/
theorem dualEvalHom_comp_subschemeStructureHom :
    dualEvalShHom L s ≫ Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s) = 0 := by
  refine SheafOfModules.Hom.ext (PresheafOfModules.hom_ext fun U =>
    ModuleCat.hom_ext (LinearMap.ext fun t => ?_))
  change (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s)).val.app U
      (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (dualEvalHom L s) U.unop t) = 0
  obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_idealSheafSection_evalAt L s U.unop t
  exact (congrArg (fun r : Γ(X, U.unop) =>
      (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s)).val.app U r) hy).symm.trans
    (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeι_app_idealSheafSection _ y)

/-- **The factorization of evaluation at `s` through the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme.**
The ideal sheaf `ℐ_{Z(s)}` is the kernel of `𝒪_X ⟶ i_* 𝒪_{Z(s)}`, and `σ` is killed by that
map, so it lifts. -/
def zeroSchemeIdealHom :
    dualModule L ⟶ Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafOfModules (zeroScheme X L s) :=
  kernel.lift (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s)) (dualEvalShHom L s)
    (dualEvalHom_comp_subschemeStructureHom L s)

/-- The factorization composed with the inclusion of the ideal sheaf is evaluation at `s`: on
sections over an open, the section of the ideal sheaf it produces is the value of `σ`. -/
theorem idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealHom (U : X.Opens) (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s)
        ((zeroSchemeIdealHom L s).val.app (op U) t) =
      evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) :=
  congrArg (fun φ : dualModule L ⟶ _ => φ.val.app (op U) t)
    (kernel.lift_ι (Stacks.Divisors.subschemeStructureHom (zeroScheme X L s)) (dualEvalShHom L s)
      (dualEvalHom_comp_subschemeStructureHom L s))

/-! ### The identification of the dual with the ideal sheaf -/

omit [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] in
/-- **Multiplication by a nonzerodivisor global section is injective on the sections over every
open.** This is [Stacks, Tag 01WY] read on sections: monicity of `𝒪_X ⟶ L` is injectivity of
each of its components, and that component is multiplication by the restriction of `s`. -/
theorem injective_smul_res_of_isRegularSection (sec : L.sections)
    (hsec : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property sec) (U : X.Opens) :
    Function.Injective fun r : Γ(X, U) =>
      r • L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : U ≤ ⊤)).op (sec.eval (op ⊤)) := by
  intro r r' h
  refine Scheme.Modules.injective_unitHomEquiv_symm_app_of_isRegularSection L sec hsec U ?_
  rw [Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_app_apply L sec U r,
    Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_app_apply L sec U r']
  exact h

variable (hreg : ∀ U : X.Opens, Function.Injective fun r : Γ(X, U) =>
  r • L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : U ≤ ⊤)).op s)

omit [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] in
include hreg in
/-- **The coordinate of a nonzerodivisor section in a rank-one dual basis is a
nonzerodivisor.** Reading the coordinate of `r • s|_W` multiplies `r` by that coordinate, and
both the coordinate map and multiplication by `s` are injective. -/
theorem isRegular_evalAt_dualBasisOne {W : X.Opens}
    (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L W) :
    IsRegular (evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)) := by
  refine isLeftRegular_iff_isRegular.mp fun r r' hrr' => ?_
  refine hreg W (injective_evalAt_dualBasisOne b ?_)
  change evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b)
      (r • L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W ≤ ⊤)).op s) =
    evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (r' • L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W ≤ ⊤)).op s)
  rw [evalAt_smul_right, evalAt_smul_right, smul_eq_mul, smul_eq_mul, mul_comm r, mul_comm r']
  exact hrr'

include hreg in
/-- **Evaluation at a nonzerodivisor section kills only the zero functional.** Over a
trivializing open the value of a functional at `s` is the coordinate of `s` — a nonzerodivisor —
times its value on the basis section, so a functional killing `s` kills the basis section and is
therefore zero; being zero is local, so this holds over every open. -/
theorem eq_zero_of_evalAt_eq_zero {U : X.Opens} (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U))
    (ht : evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) = 0) : t = 0 := by
  refine Stacks.SheafOfModules.eq_zero_of_locally_zero' (dualModule L) t fun y hy => ?_
  obtain ⟨V, hyV, hVU, ⟨b⟩⟩ := exists_affineOpen_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible L y hy
  refine ⟨(V : X.Opens), homOfLE hVU, hyV, ?_⟩
  have hres : evalAt L ((dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op t)
      (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) = 0 := by
    rw [evalAt_res_top L s hVU t, ht, map_zero]
  have hmul := evalAt_eq_mul_evalAt_dualBasisSection b
    ((dualSheaf L).presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op t) (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)
  exact b.eq_zero_of_eval_e _
    ((isRegular_evalAt_dualBasisOne L s hreg b).left
      ((hmul.symm.trans hres).trans (mul_zero _).symm))

include hreg in
/-- Evaluation at a nonzerodivisor section is injective on the sections of the dual over every
open. -/
theorem injective_evalAt_of_isRegular (U : X.Opens) :
    Function.Injective fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U) =>
      evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) := by
  exact injective_evalAt_of_eq_zero L _ fun t => eq_zero_of_evalAt_eq_zero L s hreg t

include hreg in
/-- The factorization through the ideal sheaf is injective on sections over every open: the
inclusion of the ideal sheaf is injective and the composite is evaluation at `s`. -/
theorem injective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app (U : X.Opens) :
    Function.Injective fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U) =>
      (zeroSchemeIdealHom L s).val.app (op U) t := by
  intro t t' h
  refine injective_evalAt_of_isRegular L s hreg U ?_
  refine (idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealHom L s U t).symm.trans ?_
  refine Eq.trans (congrArg (Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s)) h) ?_
  exact idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealHom L s U t'

/-- **The local equation of the zero scheme in a rank-one dual basis.** On an affine open
carrying a rank-one dual basis of `L`, the defining ideal of `Z(s)` is generated by the
coordinate of `s`. This is `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme_ideal_of_trivialization` fed the
trivialization `AlgebraicGeometry.dualBasisOneCoordEquiv`. -/
theorem zeroScheme_ideal_eq_span_evalAt (V : X.affineOpens)
    (b : Stacks.SheafOfModules.DualBasisOne X.sheaf.obj
      (structureSheafRing X.toRingedSpace).property
      L (V : X.Opens)) :
    (zeroScheme X L s).ideal V =
      Ideal.span ({evalAt L (dualBasisCoord L b) (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)} :
        Set Γ(X, (V : X.Opens))) :=
  zeroScheme_ideal_of_trivialization X L s V (dualBasisOneCoordEquiv b) _ rfl

include hreg in
/-- **The factorization through the ideal sheaf is surjective on sections over every open.**
On an affine open carrying a rank-one dual basis the ideal of the zero scheme is generated by
the coordinate `c` of `s`, so a section of the ideal sheaf there is `d · c` for some `d` and is
the image of `d` times the dual coordinate; the local preimages then glue, because the
factorization is injective on sections. -/
theorem surjective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app (U : X.Opens) :
    Function.Surjective fun t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U) =>
      (zeroSchemeIdealHom L s).val.app (op U) t := by
  intro y
  refine exists_app_eq_of_locally (dualModule L).isSheaf
    (Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafOfModules (zeroScheme X L s)).isSheaf
    (zeroSchemeIdealHom L s).val
    (fun V => injective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app L s hreg V) y fun z hz => ?_
  obtain ⟨V, hzV, hVU, ⟨b⟩⟩ := exists_affineOpen_dualBasisOne_of_isInvertible L z hz
  obtain ⟨V, hV⟩ := V
  refine ⟨V, hVU, hzV, ?_⟩
  have hmem : Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s)
      ((Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafOfModules (zeroScheme X L s)).val.resLE hVU y) ∈
      (zeroScheme X L s).ideal ⟨V, hV⟩ :=
    (Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection _ ⟨V, hV⟩ _).2 ⟨_, rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := Ideal.mem_span_singleton.1
    ((zeroScheme_ideal_eq_span_evalAt L s ⟨V, hV⟩ b) ▸ hmem)
  refine ⟨d • (dualBasisCoord L b), Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection_injective _ ?_⟩
  refine (idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealHom L s V (d • (dualBasisCoord L b))).trans ?_
  rw [hd, evalAt_smul, smul_eq_mul, mul_comm]

include hreg in
/-- **The dual of an invertible module is the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme of a
nonzerodivisor section.** Evaluation at `s` is injective on sections and hits every section of
the ideal sheaf, so it is an isomorphism `L^∨ ≅ ℐ_{Z(s)}`. -/
theorem isIso_zeroSchemeIdealHom : IsIso (zeroSchemeIdealHom L s) :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_of_bijective_app (zeroSchemeIdealHom L s) fun V =>
    ⟨injective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app L s hreg V.unop,
      surjective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app L s hreg V.unop⟩

/-! ### The invertible sheaf of the zero scheme of a regular section -/

/-- **The value of the canonical section `1_D` on a section of the ideal sheaf** is that section
read as a section of the structure sheaf: `1_D` *is* the ideal inclusion
([Stacks, Tag 01WX] (2)). -/
theorem dualPairing_canonicalSection (I : X.IdealSheafData) (W : Over (⊤ : X.Opens))
    (y : Γ(idealSheafModule I, W.left)) :
    dualPairing (idealSheafModule I) ((Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' I).val (op ⊤)) W y =
      Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection I y := rfl

/-- **The factorization `L^∨ ⟶ ℐ_{Z(s)}` of evaluation at `s`, as a morphism of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules`.** This is `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroSchemeIdealHom`; only the
spelling of its type differs. -/
def zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom : dualSheaf L ⟶ idealSheafModule (zeroScheme X L s) :=
  zeroSchemeIdealHom L s

/-- The factorization composed with the inclusion of the ideal sheaf is evaluation at `s`. -/
theorem idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom (U : X.Opens) (t : Γ(dualSheaf L, U)) :
    Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s)
        (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s) U t) =
      evalAt L t (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) :=
  idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealHom L s U t

include hreg in
/-- **The dual of an invertible module is the ideal sheaf of the zero scheme of a nonzerodivisor
section**, in the spelling of `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules`. -/
theorem isIso_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom : IsIso (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s) :=
  Scheme.Modules.Hom.isIso_iff_isIso_app.mpr fun U =>
    (ConcreteCategory.isIso_iff_bijective _).mpr
      ⟨injective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app L s hreg U, surjective_zeroSchemeIdealHom_app L s hreg U⟩

omit [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] in
/-- The value on a section of `ℐ_D` of the image of `s` under the canonical map to the double
dual, transported along the dual of a morphism `ℐ_D ⟶ L^∨`. -/
theorem dualMap_toDoubleDual_pairing {I : X.IdealSheafData}
    (ι : idealSheafModule I ⟶ dualSheaf L) (W : Over (⊤ : X.Opens))
    (y : Γ(idealSheafModule I, W.left)) :
    dualPairing (idealSheafModule I)
        (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L ≫ dualMap ι) ⊤ s) W y =
      evalAt L (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app ι W.left y)
        (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) := by
  refine (dualPairing_dualMap ι (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L) ⊤ s) W y).trans ?_
  exact congrArg (fun z : Γ(L, W.left) =>
    evalAt L (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app ι W.left y) z) (presheaf_map_hom_eq L s W)

/-- **The section identity.** If `ι : ℐ_{Z(s)} ⟶ L^∨` is a section of the factorization of
evaluation at `s`, then the composite `L ⟶ L^∨∨ ⟶ 𝒪_X(Z(s))` carries `s` to the canonical
section `1_{Z(s)}`: the value of the image of `s` on a section `y` of the ideal sheaf is
`ι(y)` evaluated at `s`, which is `y` itself. -/
theorem toDoubleDual_dualMap_eq_canonicalSection
    (ι : idealSheafModule (zeroScheme X L s) ⟶ dualSheaf L)
    (hι : ι ≫ zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s = 𝟙 _) :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (toDoubleDual L ≫ dualMap ι) ⊤ s =
      (Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' (zeroScheme X L s)).val (op ⊤) := by
  refine dualPairing_ext (F := idealSheafModule (zeroScheme X L s)) fun W y => ?_
  have h := congrArg (fun φ => Scheme.Modules.Hom.app φ W.left y) hι
  have h4 : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s) W.left
      (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app ι W.left y) = y := h
  refine (dualMap_toDoubleDual_pairing L s ι W y).trans ?_
  rw [dualPairing_canonicalSection]
  exact (idealSheafSection_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s W.left
      (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app ι W.left y)).symm.trans
    (congrArg (Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection (zeroScheme X L s)) h4)

include hreg in
/-- **An invertible module with a nonzerodivisor global section is the invertible sheaf of the
zero scheme of that section.** The isomorphism is the composite of the canonical map to the
double dual — an isomorphism because `L` is invertible — with the dual of the identification
`ℐ_{Z(s)} ≅ L^∨` of `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom`. -/
def invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme : L ≅ Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' (zeroScheme X L s) :=
  have _h1 := isIso_toDoubleDual_of_isInvertible L
  have _h2 := isIso_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s hreg
  asIso (toDoubleDual L) ≪≫ dualMapIso (asIso (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s)).symm

include hreg in
/-- **Under `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme` the section `s` becomes the
canonical section `1_{Z(s)}`.** -/
theorem invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme_section :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme L s hreg).hom ⊤ s =
      (Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' (zeroScheme X L s)).val (op ⊤) :=
  have _h1 := isIso_toDoubleDual_of_isInvertible L
  have _h2 := isIso_zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s hreg
  toDoubleDual_dualMap_eq_canonicalSection L s (asIso (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s)).inv
    (asIso (zeroSchemeIdealModuleHom L s)).inv_hom_id

end ZeroScheme

/-! ### The regular-section form -/

section RegularSection

variable (M : X.Modules)
  [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property M] (t : M.sections)
  (ht : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property t)

include ht in
/-- **An invertible module with a nonzerodivisor global section is the invertible sheaf of the
zero scheme of that section.**

Let `M` be an invertible `𝒪_X`-module and `t` a global section which is a nonzerodivisor
([Stacks, Tag 01WY]). Then `E = Z(t)` ([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]) is an effective Cartier divisor
([Stacks, Tag 02OR] (4),
`AlgebraicGeometry.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff_isRegularSection`) and this is a
canonical isomorphism `M ≅ 𝒪_X(E)`; under it `t` becomes the canonical section `1_E`, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection_section`.

## No Stacks attribute is attached

The pair of statements is the *existence* half of the clause of
[Stacks, Tag 01X0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01X0) which goes from a regular section
of an invertible sheaf to an effective Cartier divisor. That clause asserts a *unique* such
isomorphism, and it is `AlgebraicGeometry.existsUnique_invertibleSheafIso_zeroScheme` in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Divisors/RegularSectionCorrespondence.lean` which carries the
attribute; this pair is its existence input. -/
def invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection :
    M ≅ Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' (zeroScheme X M (t.eval (op ⊤))) :=
  invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme M (t.eval (op ⊤))
    (injective_smul_res_of_isRegularSection M t ht)

include ht in
/-- **Under `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection` the section `t`
becomes the canonical section of the invertible sheaf of its zero scheme.** This and the
isomorphism together are the existence half of the regular-section clause of
[Stacks, Tag 01X0]; see that declaration for why no attribute is attached. -/
theorem invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection_section :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app
        (invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection M t ht).hom ⊤ (t.eval (op ⊤)) =
      (Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' (zeroScheme X M (t.eval (op ⊤)))).val (op ⊤) :=
  invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme_section M (t.eval (op ⊤))
    (injective_smul_res_of_isRegularSection M t ht)

end RegularSection

end AlgebraicGeometry

end
